course: chemistry
lesson: 35
title: Famous discoveries told as stories
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Great discoveries are often stories of patience and luck. In 1928 Alexander Fleming came back from holiday to a dish of germs he had not cleaned, saw a blob of mould, and noticed that the germs around it had died: that mould gave the world penicillin.
explain: Other discoveries came from careful work: Antoine Lavoisier weighed everything before and after burning and showed that nothing is lost, and Marie Curie spent years separating tiny amounts of new elements from tonnes of rock.
explain: Some came from a clever idea: Dmitri Mendeleev wrote each element on a card, laid them out like a game of patience, and saw a pattern so strong that he could predict elements nobody had found yet.
explain: Good scientists check stories as well as facts. Some famous tales, like Archimedes running naked through the streets shouting "Eureka!", may not have happened, and some scientists, like Rosalind Franklin, did not get fair credit at first.
fact: Marie Curie discovered two new elements, polonium and radium, separating tiny amounts of them from tonnes of ore; her notebooks are still radioactive and are kept in lead-lined boxes.
fact: In 1928 Alexander Fleming noticed that mould on a forgotten dish killed bacteria; the mould made penicillin, and other scientists took over ten years to turn it into a medicine.
fact: Antoine Lavoisier showed by careful weighing that matter is not lost when things burn or react; he also named oxygen and hydrogen.
fact: Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the elements into the periodic table, left gaps for elements not yet found and predicted them; they were found later.
fact: Archimedes noticed water rising as he got into his bath and saw how to test a crown: an object pushes aside its own volume of water; the "Eureka" run may be a legend.
fact: Joseph Priestley found how to put gas into water and made the first soda water, using gas from a brewery; he also discovered oxygen, as Carl Scheele did on his own.
fact: Humphry Davy made a safety lamp for coal miners in 1815: a metal mesh around the flame stops it lighting explosive gas in the mine, and it saved many lives.
fact: For centuries alchemists tried to turn lead into gold; they failed, because chemistry cannot change one element into another, but they invented many lab tools and methods.
fact: Rosalind Franklin's X-ray picture, Photo 51, helped show the shape of DNA; her part was overlooked at first, and she died before the Nobel Prize for DNA was given.
fact: Charles Goodyear found in 1839 that heating rubber with sulphur makes it strong; before that rubber went sticky in summer and cracked in winter.
fact: The story that Goodyear dropped rubber on a hot stove by accident is often told, but the details are not certain.
exercise: Which two elements did Marie Curie discover? | Polonium and radium | Oxygen and hydrogen | Gold and silver | Iron and carbon
exercise: Why are Marie Curie's notebooks kept in lead-lined boxes? | They are still radioactive | They are made of real gold leaf | They would fall apart in the light | They are written in secret code
exercise: What did Alexander Fleming notice on his forgotten dish? | Mould had killed the germs around it | The whole dish had slowly turned to gold | The germs had grown into a mushroom | The glass had cracked by itself
exercise: What medicine came from Fleming's mould? | Penicillin | Aspirin tablets | Vitamin C pills | Cough syrup
exercise: What did Lavoisier show by careful weighing? | Matter is not lost when things burn | Burning things always weigh nothing | Air weighs more than water | Metals get lighter when cold
exercise: Which elements did Lavoisier name? | Oxygen and hydrogen | Radium and polonium | Gold and lead | Neon and helium
exercise: How did Mendeleev find the pattern of the periodic table? | He laid elements out on cards like a game | He dreamt it in one night without any work | He copied it from an old Greek book | He found it carved in a cave
exercise: Why did Mendeleev leave gaps in his table? | For elements not found yet | Because he had run out of ink | To make the table look pretty | For elements he did not like
exercise: What did Archimedes notice in his bath? | The water rose as he got in | The water turned to pure gold | The bath got much lighter at once | The soap floated up to the top
exercise: What did Joseph Priestley make by putting gas into water? | The first soda water | The first ice cream | The first soap | The first bottle of ink
exercise: Why was Humphry Davy's lamp important? | Its mesh stopped the flame setting off mine gas | It was the very first electric light bulb in a home | It lit up the streets of London | It could burn under water
exercise: Why could alchemists never turn lead into gold? | Chemistry cannot change one element into another | They never had enough lead | Gold is not really an element | They forgot to heat it long enough in their furnaces
exercise: What useful things did alchemists leave behind? | Lab tools and methods | Piles of real gold | The first periodic table | Cures for every illness
exercise: What did Rosalind Franklin's Photo 51 help show? | The shape of DNA | The inside of an atom | The surface of the Moon | The bones of a dinosaur
exercise: What did Charles Goodyear find out about rubber? | Heating it with sulphur makes it strong | Freezing it solid makes it bounce higher | Wetting it makes it waterproof | Rubbing it makes it magnetic
exercise: What was rubber like before Goodyear's discovery? | Sticky in summer and cracked in winter | Hard as stone all year round | Perfect in every kind of hot or cold weather | Bright blue and very shiny
test: Leo: "Fleming made penicillin into a medicine the very next week". What's true? | Others took over ten years to make it a medicine | He's right, it took just one week from dish to pill | It was never made into a medicine | Fleming made it from a bath sponge
test: A cartoon alchemist turns a lead pipe into gold with a potion. What's true? | Chemistry cannot change lead into gold | It works if the potion is strong enough | It works only with very old lead | It works on a full moon only
test: "Archimedes ran naked down the street shouting Eureka, that's history innit". What's true? | It may be a legend; the water idea is real | It is certain, it was filmed at the time | It never happened, and he had no bath | He shouted it in English
test: If Mendeleev hadn't left gaps in his table, what would have been harder? | Predicting elements nobody had found yet | Counting up all of the known elements one by one | Writing on cards | Naming oxygen and hydrogen
test: A burnt log seems to "disappear" into a small pile of ash. What would Lavoisier say? | The rest went into the air as gas; nothing is lost | It really disappeared into nothing | The ash weighs exactly the same as the log | Burning makes matter vanish forever
test: Gran: "fizzy drinks were invented by a drinks factory". Who really made the first soda water? | Joseph Priestley, a scientist | Marie Curie, in Paris | Alexander Fleming, by accident | Mendeleev, with his cards
test: Miners long ago lit their way with open flames. Why was that so dangerous? | The flame could set off gas in the mine | Open flames made the coal disappear | The flames made the air too cold | The light scared the pit ponies away
test: "Rosalind Franklin got loads of credit for DNA right away". What's true? | Her part was overlooked at first | She got the Nobel Prize all by herself | She was the only one who worked on it | She discovered penicillin as well
test: Your mate: "rubber's always been bouncy and fine, nobody had to invent anything". What's true? | Before Goodyear rubber went sticky or cracked | Rubber has never changed at all | Rubber was invented in a factory | Rubber came from oil, so no change
test: Why is it worth telling the true story of a discovery, not just the legend? | The facts may differ from the famous tale | Legends are always completely true in every detail | Only legends teach real science | True stories are always shorter
test: "Marie Curie's old notebooks are just paper, read them anywhere". What's true? | They are still radioactive and kept with care | They are ordinary paper, safe anywhere | They glow in the dark like torches | They were all burnt long ago
exam: Identify the substance whose discovery is attributed to the observation by Alexander Fleming of mould upon a neglected culture dish in 1928. | Penicillin | Radium | Oxygen | Soda water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contribution of Antoine Lavoisier? | He demonstrated by weighing that matter is conserved in reactions | He arranged the elements upon cards into a table | He isolated polonium and radium from ore | He photographed the structure of DNA
exam: It is given that Dmitri Mendeleev left vacant positions within his periodic table. Ascertain the purpose of the said vacancies. | To accommodate elements not yet discovered | To indicate elements known to be poisonous | To separate metals from gases | To mark elements discovered by alchemists
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the endeavours of the alchemists. | They failed to transmute lead into gold but devised many laboratory techniques | They succeeded in transmuting lead into gold | They discovered the structure of DNA | They invented the miners' safety lamp
